{
  "dom_id": "step-q346443",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The following table shows the water drainage rate of a reservoir over a period of $8$ hours, starting at $2\\,\\text{p.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{gallons/hour})$ | $2$ | $k$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total water drained from $2\\,\\text{p.m}$ to $10\\,\\text{p.m.}$ has a value of $30$ gallons, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The area $A$ under the curve (rate vs. time) can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R(4)+R(6)+R(8)+R(10)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of the rate at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+4+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+12) \\\\  & =2k+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total water drained from $2\\,\\text{p.m}$ to $10\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total drainage is $30$ gallons, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+24 & =30 \\\\ 2k & =30-24 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
